/* WISEBYTE LESSONS · Money & Economics (batch z) */
WB.L("decimal-currency-1966", "money", "beginner", "Dollar Bill and the day Australia went decimal", "On one Monday in 1966, a whole nation swapped pounds, shillings and pence for dollars and cents. Almost nobody wanted it called the dollar at first.",
  [
    ["Pounds, shillings and pence", "Before 1966 Australians did their sums in a system based on twelves and twenties.",
      "For most of its history Australia counted money the British way. A pound was worth 20 shillings, and each shilling was worth 12 pence, so a pound held 240 pennies. Prices were written in a clumsy shorthand such as £2 7s 6d, and adding up a shopping bill meant constant carrying of twelves and twenties. Australia had issued its own pound since 1910, but the arithmetic was inherited from medieval England. By the 1950s businesses, banks and schools were complaining. Children spent hours learning to convert pence into shillings, and the new adding machines and early computers suited tens far better. Many countries had long since gone decimal, and a government committee set up in 1959 recommended Australia follow."],
    ["Royal, emu or ming?", "The government's first choice of name, the ‘royal’, was so unpopular it was quietly dropped.",
      "Choosing a name proved harder than doing the maths. The public sent in thousands of suggestions, from the austral and the kanga to the emu and the ming, a cheeky nod to Prime Minister Robert Menzies' nickname. In 1963 the government announced that the new unit would be called the royal. The reaction was swift and scornful. Newspapers mocked it, and many people felt it sounded old-fashioned for a young country building its own identity. Within months the government backed down and settled on the dollar, a name already used in the United States, Canada and elsewhere, and short enough to say easily. The fuss is a reminder that money is not just maths. It carries national pride."],
    ["C-Day, 14 February 1966", "The switch happened on a single day, backed by a huge public education campaign.",
      "The changeover was set for Monday 14 February 1966, nicknamed C-Day. To prepare people, the Decimal Currency Board ran one of the biggest public information campaigns Australia had seen. Its star was a cartoon character called Dollar Bill, and a catchy jingle sung to the tune of the bush ballad ‘Click Go the Shears’ explained the change on radio and television. The conversion rule was simple: ten shillings became one dollar, so one pound became two dollars. Banks closed briefly beforehand to prepare, and thousands of cash registers and machines had to be converted or replaced. Old and new money circulated side by side for a while, with shopkeepers giving change in decimal currency to drain the old coins from circulation."],
    ["Animals in your pocket", "The new coins carried Australian wildlife, a design that has lasted for decades.",
      "The coin designs came from Stuart Devlin, a young Australian designer who later became a famous silversmith in London. Instead of kings and coats of arms on every coin, he chose native animals. The one cent showed a feathertail glider, the two cent a frilled-neck lizard, the five cent an echidna, the ten cent a lyrebird and the twenty cent a platypus. The fifty cent carried the national coat of arms. The monarch stayed on the other side, as is still the custom. These designs became so familiar that they have stayed largely unchanged through many decades, even as portraits of the monarch were updated. For many Australians, the platypus on the twenty is as recognisable as any flag."],
    ["The coin worth more than its face", "The first round fifty cent coin contained so much silver it was worth melting down.",
      "One coin did not last long. The original 1966 fifty cent piece was round and made of 80 per cent silver. That seemed reasonable at first, but the world price of silver climbed during the 1960s, and soon the metal inside the coin was worth more than fifty cents. People began hoarding them rather than spending them, a textbook case of what economists call Gresham's law: when two kinds of money circulate, the one with more valuable content tends to disappear into drawers, leaving the cheaper one in use. The government withdrew the round coin and in 1969 introduced the distinctive twelve-sided fifty cent piece, made of copper and nickel, which is still in use today."],
    ["Plastic notes and vanishing coins", "Australia kept innovating, pioneering polymer banknotes and later scrapping its smallest coins.",
      "The story did not stop in 1966. In 1988, for the bicentenary of European settlement, the Reserve Bank issued a ten dollar note printed on plastic polymer instead of paper. It was developed in Australia, partly to defeat counterfeiters, since a clear window and other features were very hard to copy. By 1996 Australia had become the first country with a full series of polymer notes, and the technology has since spread to many countries. Meanwhile inflation slowly shrank the value of small coins. In 1992 the one and two cent coins were withdrawn, and cash totals have been rounded to the nearest five cents ever since. Card payments, of course, are still charged to the exact cent."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Under the old system, how many pence made up one pound?", ["100", "120", "240", "20"], 2, "A pound held 20 shillings and each shilling held 12 pence, so a pound was 240 pence."],
    [1, "What name did the government first announce for the new currency in 1963?", ["The royal", "The austral", "The emu", "The sterling"], 0, "The government announced the royal, but public mockery led it to switch to the dollar."],
    [2, "What was the conversion rule on C-Day?", ["One pound became one dollar", "Ten shillings became one dollar", "One shilling became one dollar", "One penny became one cent"], 1, "Ten shillings equalled one dollar, so one pound became two dollars."],
    [4, "Why did the round 1966 fifty cent coin disappear from circulation?", ["It was too easy to forge", "It was too heavy to carry", "Its silver came to be worth more than fifty cents", "It showed the wrong monarch"], 2, "Rising silver prices meant the metal was worth more than the face value, so people hoarded the coins, an example of Gresham's law."],
    [5, "What happened to Australia's one and two cent coins in 1992?", ["They were made of polymer", "They were withdrawn and cash totals rounded to five cents", "They were redesigned with new animals", "They became collectors' gold coins"], 1, "The coins were withdrawn and cash totals have since been rounded to the nearest five cents."]
  ],
  { added: "2026-10-26" });

WB.L("exchange-rates-aussie-dollar", "money", "beginner", "Why the Aussie dollar rises and falls", "In 2001 an Australian dollar bought less than 50 US cents. A decade later it bought more than a US dollar. Here is what moves a currency.",
  [
    ["A price like any other", "An exchange rate is simply the price of one currency in terms of another.",
      "When you hear that the Australian dollar is ‘at 65 US cents’, it means one Australian dollar can be swapped for 65 American cents. An exchange rate is a price, and like other prices it is set by supply and demand. Every day, importers, exporters, tourists, investors and banks buy and sell currencies, and the trading in currency markets is enormous, measured in trillions of US dollars a day worldwide. If more people want Australian dollars, perhaps to buy Australian iron ore or Australian bonds, the price rises. If more people want to sell them, it falls. The Australian dollar is one of the most heavily traded currencies in the world, often nicknamed the Aussie."],
    ["Floating the dollar in 1983", "Australia let its currency float freely in December 1983, one of the biggest economic reforms in its history.",
      "It was not always this way. For decades Australia's currency was pegged, first to the British pound, then to the US dollar, and from 1974 to a basket of currencies that officials adjusted. The government and Reserve Bank effectively decided the rate, and strict controls limited how much money could flow in or out of the country. Defending the peg became increasingly difficult as money sloshed around the world. In December 1983 the Hawke government, with Treasurer Paul Keating, floated the dollar and removed most exchange controls, letting the market set the price. It was a dramatic shift. Since then the Reserve Bank has only occasionally stepped into the market, usually during periods of severe disorder."],
    ["A commodity currency", "The Aussie dollar often moves with the prices of the minerals and farm goods Australia exports.",
      "Australia sells huge quantities of iron ore, coal, gas, gold, wheat and beef to the world. When those commodity prices climb, foreign buyers need more Australian dollars to pay for them, and the currency tends to rise. That is why traders call the Aussie a commodity currency. The mining boom of the 2000s is the clearest example. China's rapid industrialisation drove up demand for Australian iron ore and coal, and the dollar climbed from a low of under 48 US cents in 2001 to above parity, more than one US dollar, in late 2010, peaking in 2011. When commodity prices later fell, the dollar fell with them. The link is not perfect, but it is strong enough to watch."],
    ["Interest rates and hot money", "Higher interest rates can attract investors, pushing a currency up.",
      "Commodities are only part of the story. Investors constantly move money around the world looking for the best return. If Australian interest rates are higher than those in the United States or Japan, investors can earn more by holding Australian dollar deposits or bonds, so they buy Australian dollars to do it. This tends to push the currency up. One famous strategy, the carry trade, involves borrowing in a low interest currency, such as the Japanese yen for much of recent history, and investing in a higher interest one. It can be profitable for years and then unwind suddenly when markets panic. That is one reason the Aussie often falls sharply during global crises, as nervous investors rush to safer currencies."],
    ["Winners and losers", "A strong dollar helps importers and travellers, while a weak one helps exporters and tourism.",
      "Whether a high dollar is good news depends on who you are. When the Aussie is strong, overseas holidays are cheaper, imported cars and electronics cost less, and Australians can buy foreign shares more cheaply. But exporters suffer, because their goods become more expensive for foreign buyers, and local manufacturers struggle to compete against cheaper imports. Universities and tourism operators also feel it, since Australia becomes a pricier place to study or visit. A weak dollar flips these effects. Overseas trips hurt, but farmers, miners and tour operators gain. This is why the exchange rate works as a kind of shock absorber for the economy, cushioning the blow when commodity prices or global conditions change."],
    ["The Big Mac test", "Comparing burger prices is a playful way to ask whether a currency is overvalued.",
      "If a currency is fairly priced, the same basket of goods should cost roughly the same everywhere once you convert the money. Economists call this idea purchasing power parity. In 1986 The Economist magazine turned it into a light-hearted gauge called the Big Mac index, comparing the price of the burger in different countries. If a Big Mac costs much more in Switzerland than in the United States after conversion, the Swiss franc looks overvalued by burger logic. The index is deliberately simple, since local wages, rents and taxes all affect burger prices. But it captures a real idea. In the long run exchange rates tend to drift towards levels that make prices broadly comparable, even if they stray widely in the short term."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What is an exchange rate?", ["A tax on foreign travel", "The price of one currency in terms of another", "The interest rate set by the central bank", "A fixed rate set by the United Nations"], 1, "An exchange rate is a price, set by supply and demand for currencies."],
    [1, "What happened to the Australian dollar in December 1983?", ["It was pegged to the US dollar", "It replaced the pound", "It was floated and the market set its price", "It was linked to the price of gold"], 2, "The Hawke government, with Treasurer Paul Keating, floated the dollar and removed most exchange controls."],
    [2, "Why is the Aussie called a commodity currency?", ["It often moves with the prices of the resources Australia exports", "It is backed by gold reserves", "It can only be used to buy commodities", "It is made of metal"], 0, "When prices for iron ore, coal and other exports rise, demand for Australian dollars rises too."],
    [3, "How can higher Australian interest rates affect the dollar?", ["They always weaken it", "They have no effect", "They can attract foreign investors, pushing the dollar up", "They force the dollar back to parity"], 2, "Investors seeking better returns buy Australian dollars to hold higher yielding deposits and bonds."],
    [4, "Who generally benefits from a weak Australian dollar?", ["Australians holidaying overseas", "Exporters and tourism operators", "Importers of foreign cars", "People buying foreign shares"], 1, "A weak dollar makes Australian goods and holidays cheaper for foreigners, helping exporters and tourism."]
  ],
  { added: "2026-10-26" });

WB.L("bank-runs", "money", "beginner", "Bank runs: when everyone wants their money at once", "Your bank does not keep your savings in a vault. Usually that is fine. When everyone turns up at once, it can be catastrophic.",
  [
    ["Where your money really is", "Banks lend out most of the money deposited with them, keeping only a fraction on hand.",
      "When you deposit money in a bank, it does not sit in a box with your name on it. Banks take deposits that customers can withdraw at any moment and use them to fund loans that will not be repaid for years, such as mortgages and business loans. They keep only a small portion as cash and reserves, betting, sensibly, that not everyone will want their money on the same day. This arrangement is the heart of banking and it is genuinely useful, turning idle savings into homes and businesses. But it creates a built-in fragility. A bank can be perfectly sound, with good loans that will be repaid, and still be unable to hand everyone their money back immediately."],
    ["The self-fulfilling panic", "A run can topple a healthy bank simply because people fear it will fall.",
      "Imagine a rumour spreads that your bank is in trouble. Even if you think the rumour is false, you have a problem. If other people believe it and withdraw their money, the bank may run out of cash, and those who wait could lose out. So the rational move is to join the queue. Everyone reasons the same way, and the fear becomes reality. Economists Douglas Diamond and Philip Dybvig described this trap in a famous 1983 model, showing how a bank run can be a self-fulfilling prophecy. Their work helped earn them a share of the 2022 Nobel Prize in economics, alongside Ben Bernanke, who studied how bank failures deepened the Great Depression."],
    ["Melbourne in the 1890s", "Australia suffered one of history's great banking crises after a property bubble burst.",
      "Australia knows this story well. In the 1880s Melbourne boomed on gold wealth and borrowed British money, and land prices soared in a speculative frenzy. Dozens of building societies and land banks lent freely against property. When the bubble burst at the end of the decade, borrowers defaulted and many of these institutions collapsed. In 1893 the crisis reached the major banks. Depositors rushed to withdraw their money, and many of the colonies' major banks suspended payments within a few weeks. Some reopened after being reorganised, but savings were frozen or lost and the colonies plunged into a deep depression that lasted for much of the decade. Melbourne took years to recover."],
    ["George Bailey's lesson", "Banks fight runs by explaining that the money is safe, just tied up in loans.",
      "The classic film It's a Wonderful Life, released in 1946, contains one of the clearest explanations of a bank run ever filmed. When panicked customers crowd into George Bailey's building and loan company demanding their savings, he explains that their money is not in a safe. It is in Joe's house and the Kennedys' house, lent out so neighbours could buy homes. If everyone insists on withdrawing at once, the whole community suffers. Bailey calms the crowd by asking each person to take only what they need. Real banks rely on something similar but more powerful: a central bank willing to lend them cash against good assets in an emergency, acting as a lender of last resort."],
    ["Deposit guarantees", "Governments now insure deposits so that ordinary savers have no reason to run.",
      "The most effective cure for bank runs has been deposit insurance. After thousands of American banks failed in the early 1930s, the United States created the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation in 1933, promising savers they would get their money back up to a limit. With that guarantee in place, there was little reason to panic, and large-scale runs became rare. Australia introduced its Financial Claims Scheme during the global financial crisis in 2008. As of the mid-2020s it protects deposits up to 250,000 dollars per account holder at each authorised bank, building society or credit union. Because the guarantee exists, the need to use it is far less likely. Confidence itself is the protection."],
    ["Runs in the digital age", "Modern runs can happen in hours, driven by phones and social media.",
      "Runs have not disappeared. In September 2007 queues formed outside branches of Britain's Northern Rock, one of the first visible signs of the global financial crisis and the first run on a British bank in well over a century. In March 2023 California's Silicon Valley Bank collapsed in a run of startling speed. Many of its customers were tech firms with deposits far above the insured limit, and as worries spread through group chats and social media, they tried to pull tens of billions of dollars in a single day using banking apps. There were no queues outside, just a flood of online transfers. The bank was closed by regulators within roughly two days, showing how quickly modern panic can spread."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why can a sound bank still be unable to pay all depositors at once?", ["It keeps all deposits in gold", "Most deposits have been lent out in long-term loans", "Banks are not allowed to hold cash", "Deposits are held by the government"], 1, "Banks lend most deposits as long-term loans and keep only a fraction available as cash."],
    [1, "What did Diamond and Dybvig show about bank runs?", ["They only happen to badly run banks", "They can be self-fulfilling, toppling even healthy banks", "They are caused by high interest rates", "They cannot happen once a country has a central bank"], 1, "Their 1983 model showed that fear of a run can rationally cause one, even at a sound bank."],
    [3, "In It's a Wonderful Life, where does George Bailey say the depositors' money is?", ["In a vault in New York", "Invested in gold", "Lent out in their neighbours' homes", "Spent on staff wages"], 2, "He explains the money is lent out to neighbours for their houses, not sitting in a safe."],
    [4, "What does Australia's Financial Claims Scheme do?", ["Guarantees deposits up to a limit at authorised institutions", "Sets the cash rate", "Insures shares against market falls", "Lends money to first home buyers"], 0, "It protects deposits, as of the mid-2020s up to 250,000 dollars per account holder at each authorised institution."],
    [5, "What made the 2023 run on Silicon Valley Bank unusual?", ["It happened over several years", "It involved only paper cheques", "It was extraordinarily fast, driven by online transfers and social media", "It was stopped by deposit insurance alone"], 2, "Customers tried to pull tens of billions of dollars in a day using apps, with panic spreading online."]
  ],
  { added: "2026-10-26" });

WB.L("medici-bank", "money", "beginner", "The Medici: the bankers who built a Renaissance", "A family of Florentine money-changers became bankers to the Pope, patrons of genius and rulers of a city. Their tricks still shape finance today.",
  [
    ["Men at the bench", "Banking in Renaissance Italy began with money-changers working at benches in the street.",
      "In the 1300s Italian cities such as Florence, Venice and Genoa were the trading hubs of Europe, awash with coins from dozens of different states. Merchants needed someone to weigh, test and swap these coins, and to move money safely across long distances. Money-changers set up at tables, and the Italian word for such a bench, banco, gave us the word bank. A banker who could not pay was said to be banca rotta, a broken bench, the root of our word bankrupt. In 1397 Giovanni di Bicci de' Medici founded the Medici Bank in Florence. Within a few decades it would become the most powerful bank in Europe."],
    ["God's banker", "The Medici grew rich by managing the finances of the Catholic Church.",
      "The Medici's greatest client was the papacy. The Church collected revenues from all over Christian Europe, from England to Poland, and needed a way to move that money to Rome without carting chests of silver across the Alps. The Medici, with their network of trusted agents, could do it. For long stretches of the 1400s they served as the main bankers to the Pope, a relationship that brought enormous profits and political influence. Their Rome branch was often the most profitable in the company. The connection lasted well beyond the bank itself. In the following century two members of the family became popes themselves, Leo X and Clement VII, a sign of how deeply the family had woven itself into the Church."],
    ["Getting around the usury ban", "The Church banned charging interest, so bankers earned it through currency exchange instead.",
      "There was an awkward problem. The Church condemned usury, which in that era meant charging interest on a loan, as a sin. So how could a bank make money? The answer was the bill of exchange. A merchant in Florence might hand over florins and receive a document payable months later in another currency, say in Bruges or London. The rates used at each end were set so that the banker made a profit, but on paper the deal was an exchange of currencies, not a loan. Theologians argued over whether this was acceptable, and the risk of exchange rate movements gave bankers a defence. In practice, interest was hidden inside the exchange."],
    ["A multinational company", "The Medici ran a network of semi-independent branches across Europe, an early model for modern firms.",
      "At its height the Medici Bank had branches in Rome, Venice, Milan, Geneva, Avignon, Bruges and London, among other places. Each was run by a branch manager who often held a share in the profits, giving him a reason to work hard, while the family in Florence kept overall control. Branches were set up as separate partnerships, which meant a disaster in one city would not automatically drag down the rest. The Medici and other Italian merchants kept careful accounts using double-entry bookkeeping, recording every transaction as both a debit and a credit so errors stood out. The method was spreading among Italian traders by the 1300s, and the friar Luca Pacioli described it in print in 1494."],
    ["Money, art and power", "Medici wealth funded some of the greatest art and architecture of the Renaissance.",
      "Giovanni's son Cosimo de' Medici used the bank's fortune to become the unofficial ruler of Florence from 1434, steering politics through allies rather than holding a crown. He was also a generous patron. He funded churches, the library of the monastery of San Marco and artists such as Donatello, and supported scholars who translated ancient Greek philosophy. His grandson Lorenzo, known as Lorenzo the Magnificent, continued the tradition, and the young Michelangelo spent time in his household. Part of this giving was genuine love of learning and beauty. Part of it was likely an attempt to win public support, and perhaps to ease consciences troubled by the Church's teaching on wealth. Either way, Florence was transformed."],
    ["The fall of the bank", "Bad loans to rulers and weaker management brought the bank down by 1494.",
      "Under Lorenzo, who was more interested in politics and poetry than ledgers, the bank weakened. Some branch managers made reckless loans to princes, who were powerful, unpredictable and hard to sue. The London branch lent heavily to King Edward IV during the Wars of the Roses and suffered heavy losses. The Bruges branch, under Tommaso Portinari, lent to Charles the Bold, Duke of Burgundy, who was killed in battle in 1477. One by one, branches closed. In 1494, when the Medici were driven out of Florence, the bank collapsed. The lesson echoes through financial history: lending to the mighty can feel safe until the mighty decide, or are unable, to pay."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Where does the word ‘bank’ come from?", ["The Latin word for gold", "The Italian word banco, meaning bench", "The name of a German banking family", "The banks of the river Arno"], 1, "Money-changers worked at benches, banco in Italian, which gave us bank, and banca rotta gave us bankrupt."],
    [1, "Who was the Medici Bank's most important client for much of the 1400s?", ["The King of France", "The papacy", "The Ottoman Sultan", "The Venetian navy"], 1, "The Medici served as main bankers to the Pope, moving Church revenues across Europe."],
    [2, "How did bankers earn a return despite the Church's ban on usury?", ["By building profits into bills of exchange between currencies", "By charging entry fees to churches", "By selling indulgences", "By ignoring the ban openly"], 0, "Bills of exchange were framed as currency swaps, with profit built into the exchange rates."],
    [3, "Why were Medici branches set up as separate partnerships?", ["To avoid all taxes", "So a disaster in one branch would not automatically sink the others", "Because the Pope required it", "So each branch could issue its own coins"], 1, "Separate partnerships limited the damage a failure in one city could do to the rest."],
    [5, "What helped bring down the Medici Bank?", ["A Florentine stock market crash", "The invention of paper money", "Risky loans to rulers such as Edward IV and Charles the Bold", "A ban on double-entry bookkeeping"], 2, "Large loans to powerful rulers went bad, and the bank collapsed in 1494 when the Medici were expelled from Florence."]
  ],
  { added: "2026-10-26" });

WB.L("behavioural-economics-money", "money", "beginner", "Behavioural economics: why we're not rational with money", "Traditional economics imagined people as cool calculators. Then psychologists started running experiments, and the calculators turned out to be human.",
  [
    ["The rational myth", "Classic economic models assumed people weigh every option logically to get the best outcome.",
      "For much of the twentieth century, economic theory leaned on a convenient character sometimes nicknamed Homo economicus. This imaginary person knows what they want, calculates costs and benefits precisely, never gets tired or tempted, and always chooses the option that serves them best. The assumption made the maths workable, and it still explains a great deal about how markets behave. But anyone who has bought something on impulse, kept a gym membership they never use or put off saving for retirement knows real people are different. Behavioural economics grew from the idea of testing how people actually decide, using experiments borrowed from psychology, and then building those findings back into economics."],
    ["Losses loom larger", "Losing something tends to hurt more than gaining the same thing feels good.",
      "In 1979 psychologists Daniel Kahneman and Amos Tversky published prospect theory, which described how people really evaluate risky choices. One of its central ideas is loss aversion. In many experiments, the pain of losing 100 dollars outweighs the pleasure of winning 100 dollars, often by something like twice as much, though estimates vary and some researchers argue the effect is smaller or depends on the situation. Loss aversion helps explain why investors hang on to falling shares, hoping to avoid admitting a loss, and why ‘don't miss out’ marketing works so well. Kahneman was awarded the Nobel Prize in economics in 2002. Tversky had died in 1996, and the prize is not given posthumously."],
    ["Money in mental jars", "We treat money differently depending on where it came from or what we have labelled it for.",
      "In theory a dollar is a dollar. In practice we sort money into mental jars. Economist Richard Thaler called this mental accounting. Someone might carry credit card debt at a high interest rate while keeping savings in a holiday fund earning far less, because the holiday money feels untouchable. A tax refund or a win at the races often gets spent more freely than wages, as if it were not quite real money. Mental accounting is not always bad. Labelling money for bills or emergencies can be a useful way to stop yourself overspending. But it can also lead to choices that cost money, so it helps to ask occasionally whether your jars still make sense together."],
    ["The mug experiment", "People tend to value things more simply because they own them.",
      "In a well-known study published in 1990, Kahneman, Thaler and Jack Knetsch handed coffee mugs to half the students in a class. Those who received mugs were asked what price they would sell for, and those without were asked what they would pay. In theory the two groups should have valued the mugs similarly. Instead, owners typically demanded around twice as much as buyers were willing to pay. This is the endowment effect. It helps explain why people overprice their own homes and cars when selling, and why free trial offers work: once something feels like yours, giving it up feels like a loss. Later research has debated how strong the effect is in different settings."],
    ["Present bias", "We tend to grab rewards now and push costs into the future, even against our own plans.",
      "Ask people whether they would prefer 100 dollars in a year or 110 dollars in a year and a week, and most will wait the extra week. Ask whether they want 100 dollars today or 110 dollars next week, and many grab the money now. The gap is the same, but the present has a special pull. Economists call this present bias. It shows up in spending, saving, diets and procrastination, and it explains why buy now, pay later deals are so tempting. The future self who has to pay feels like a stranger. One response is to make good choices automatic, so your present self does not have to win the argument every time."],
    ["Nudges and super", "Small changes to how choices are presented can change behaviour, without banning anything.",
      "If people are predictably irrational, can choices be designed to help them? In their 2008 book Nudge, Richard Thaler and Cass Sunstein argued that defaults matter enormously. If workers are automatically enrolled in a retirement plan and must opt out, far more end up saving than if they must opt in. Thaler and Shlomo Benartzi designed a scheme called Save More Tomorrow, in which workers commit in advance to saving part of future pay rises, sidestepping both present bias and loss aversion. Thaler received the Nobel Prize in 2017. Australia's compulsory superannuation, introduced in 1992, goes further than a nudge, since employers must contribute, but it rests on a similar insight about how hard saving is."]
  ],
  [
    [1, "What is loss aversion?", ["Avoiding all investments", "Feeling losses more strongly than equal gains", "Preferring to save rather than spend", "Fear of losing a wallet"], 1, "In many experiments losses hurt roughly twice as much as equal gains please, though estimates vary."],
    [2, "Someone keeps a holiday fund while paying high interest on a credit card. Which idea does this illustrate?", ["Mental accounting", "The endowment effect", "Inflation", "Gresham's law"], 0, "Treating money as separate jars, rather than as interchangeable, is mental accounting."],
    [3, "In the mug experiment, what did owners of the mugs tend to do?", ["Give them away for free", "Ask about the same price buyers offered", "Demand around twice what buyers would pay", "Refuse to answer"], 2, "Owners valued the mugs more simply because they owned them, the endowment effect."],
    [4, "Why do buy now, pay later deals appeal so strongly?", ["They are always the cheapest option", "Present bias makes immediate rewards feel more important than future costs", "They improve credit scores", "They are required by law"], 1, "Present bias pulls us towards rewards now while the future cost feels distant."],
    [5, "What did Thaler and Sunstein argue about defaults?", ["They have little effect", "Automatic enrolment with an opt-out greatly increases saving", "People should always be forced to choose", "Defaults should be banned"], 1, "When saving is the default, far more people end up saving than when they must opt in."]
  ],
  { added: "2026-10-26" });

WB.L("gold-standard-bretton-woods", "money", "intermediate", "The gold standard and the end of Bretton Woods", "For generations, money was promised to be as good as gold. Then on a Sunday night in 1971, an American president broke the link with a television address.",
  [
    ["Money tied to metal", "Under the gold standard a currency could be swapped for a fixed amount of gold.",
      "For much of the nineteenth and early twentieth centuries, the world's major currencies were tied to gold. Under the gold standard a government promised that its paper money could be exchanged for a fixed weight of gold on demand. Britain led the way. Its pound was effectively linked to gold from the early 1700s, partly thanks to a gold price set in 1717 while Isaac Newton was Master of the Mint, and formally from 1821. By the 1870s Germany, the United States and many others had joined. Because each currency was fixed to gold, they were fixed to each other too, making international trade and lending more predictable. The classical gold standard lasted until the First World War."],
    ["The golden straitjacket", "Gold kept money stable but tied governments' hands during downturns.",
      "The gold standard had a strong appeal. A government could not simply print money to pay its bills, because it had to hold enough gold to back its promises, so long-run inflation stayed low. But this discipline came at a cost. The money supply depended on how much gold a country held, not on what the economy needed. When gold flowed out, a country often had to raise interest rates and squeeze its economy, even in a slump. Prices could fall for years, making debts harder to repay. The system also depended on new gold discoveries. Gold rushes in California, Australia and South Africa expanded the world's money supply in ways that had nothing to do with economic policy."],
    ["Gold and the Great Depression", "Countries that abandoned gold earlier generally recovered from the Depression sooner.",
      "After the First World War, countries tried to rebuild the gold standard. In 1925 Britain, with Winston Churchill as Chancellor of the Exchequer, returned to gold at the pre-war rate, which most economists now agree overvalued the pound and hurt British industry. John Maynard Keynes attacked the decision in a pamphlet called The Economic Consequences of Mr Churchill. When the Great Depression struck, the gold standard helped spread deflation from country to country, as central banks raised rates to defend their gold. Britain left gold in 1931 and the United States effectively devalued in 1933. Research by economic historians, including Barry Eichengreen and Ben Bernanke, found that countries leaving gold earlier tended to recover faster."],
    ["Bretton Woods, 1944", "Allied nations designed a new system with the US dollar at its centre, linked to gold.",
      "In July 1944, while the Second World War still raged, delegates from 44 Allied nations met at a hotel in Bretton Woods, New Hampshire. Led by Keynes for Britain and Harry Dexter White for the United States, they designed a new monetary order to avoid the chaos of the 1930s. Under the Bretton Woods system, other currencies were pegged to the US dollar, and the dollar was convertible into gold at 35 dollars an ounce, but only for foreign governments and central banks, not ordinary citizens. Countries could adjust their pegs in serious circumstances. The conference also created the International Monetary Fund and what became the World Bank, both still central to the global economy today."],
    ["The Triffin dilemma", "The system needed ever more dollars abroad, which slowly undermined faith in the gold promise.",
      "Bretton Woods carried a hidden contradiction, identified by economist Robert Triffin around 1960. The world needed a growing supply of dollars to finance expanding trade, and those dollars could only come from the United States spending, investing and lending abroad. But the more dollars piled up overseas, the less likely it was that America held enough gold to honour them all. Through the 1960s, war spending in Vietnam and domestic programmes widened the gap. Foreign governments, notably France under Charles de Gaulle, began converting dollars into gold, and American gold reserves shrank. By the end of the decade, there were far more dollars held abroad than the gold in American vaults could cover at 35 dollars an ounce."],
    ["The Nixon shock", "In August 1971 the US suspended the dollar's convertibility into gold, ending the gold link for good.",
      "On Sunday 15 August 1971, President Richard Nixon went on television and announced that the United States would temporarily stop converting dollars into gold for foreign governments. He also imposed wage and price controls and a surcharge on imports. The suspension was never lifted. Attempts to patch together new fixed rates collapsed, and by 1973 the major currencies were floating against each other. Since then, the world's money has been fiat money, valuable because governments declare it legal tender and people trust it, not because it can be exchanged for metal. Some still call for a return to gold, but most economists argue that the flexibility to respond to crises is worth the loss of the golden anchor."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Under a gold standard, what could you do with paper money?", ["Exchange it for a fixed amount of gold", "Use it only within one city", "Exchange it for shares", "Nothing beyond buying goods"], 0, "The government promised paper money could be converted into a fixed weight of gold."],
    [2, "What did economic historians find about countries that left gold early in the Great Depression?", ["They suffered longer depressions", "They tended to recover sooner", "They were invaded", "They saw no difference"], 1, "Leaving gold freed countries to ease monetary policy, and early leavers generally recovered faster."],
    [3, "Under Bretton Woods, who could convert US dollars into gold at 35 dollars an ounce?", ["Any American citizen", "Foreign governments and central banks", "Only the IMF", "Nobody"], 1, "Convertibility was available to foreign governments and central banks, not ordinary citizens."],
    [4, "What was the Triffin dilemma?", ["Gold was too heavy to transport", "The world needed more dollars abroad, which eroded confidence in their gold backing", "The IMF could not agree on a president", "Exchange rates changed every day"], 1, "The growing pile of overseas dollars made the gold promise less and less credible."],
    [5, "What did President Nixon announce on 15 August 1971?", ["A return to the gold standard", "A new currency", "A suspension of dollar convertibility into gold", "The founding of the IMF"], 2, "The Nixon shock suspended convertibility, and by 1973 major currencies were floating."]
  ],
  { added: "2026-10-26" });

WB.L("global-financial-crisis", "money", "intermediate", "The global financial crisis of 2008", "It began with American home loans that should never have been made. Within months, some of the world's biggest banks were failing and governments were scrambling to stop a second Great Depression.",
  [
    ["A housing boom built on credit", "In the early 2000s US house prices soared, fuelled by easy loans to risky borrowers.",
      "In the early 2000s American house prices rose rapidly for years. Interest rates were low after the dot-com crash, and lenders competed fiercely for business. Many began offering subprime mortgages to borrowers with weak credit histories, sometimes with little checking of income at all, and often with low introductory rates that would jump later. The thinking was that rising house prices would bail everyone out. If a borrower struggled, they could refinance or sell at a profit. Buyers, lenders and investors all assumed prices would keep climbing, or at least would not fall across the whole country at once. When prices peaked around 2006 and began to fall, that assumption collapsed, and defaults began to climb."],
    ["Slicing and dicing the risk", "Mortgages were bundled into complex securities and sold around the world.",
      "Many lenders did not keep the loans they made. Instead, banks bundled thousands of mortgages into securities and sold them to investors, a process called securitisation. These were then repackaged again into more complex products, such as collateralised debt obligations, which split the income into layers of different risk. Credit rating agencies gave many of the top layers their highest AAA rating, signalling they were extremely safe. Pension funds, banks and councils around the world bought them. Because lenders could sell the loans on, some had less reason to care whether borrowers could repay. And because the products were so complex, few investors truly understood how much risk they held, or where it really sat."],
    ["Leverage and the domino effect", "Banks had borrowed heavily, so small losses could wipe them out.",
      "The danger was magnified by leverage. Many investment banks had borrowed large sums relative to their own capital, often around thirty dollars or more for every dollar of their own, and much of that borrowing was short term. When mortgage securities lost value, a bank's thin cushion of capital could vanish quickly. Lenders grew nervous and refused to roll over loans, and banks stopped trusting each other. In March 2008 the investment bank Bear Stearns was rescued through a sale to JPMorgan Chase with support from the US Federal Reserve. Then, in September, came the shock that turned a crisis into a panic. The authorities chose not to rescue Lehman Brothers, a 158-year-old investment bank."],
    ["September 2008", "Lehman Brothers' collapse triggered a global panic and frozen credit markets.",
      "On 15 September 2008 Lehman Brothers filed for bankruptcy, the largest in US history, with assets of more than 600 billion dollars. Within days the insurance giant AIG, which had sold enormous amounts of protection against mortgage defaults, needed a massive government rescue. A large money market fund that held Lehman debt ‘broke the buck’, falling below one dollar a share, sparking runs on similar funds. Lending between banks almost seized up around the world, and companies struggled to borrow even for routine needs. Share markets plunged. Governments and central banks responded with unprecedented measures, including the 700 billion dollar US rescue program known as TARP, emergency lending and guarantees to stop a total collapse of the financial system."],
    ["How Australia dodged recession", "Fast stimulus, sharp rate cuts, sound banks and Chinese demand helped Australia avoid a recession.",
      "Australia came through the crisis better than almost any other advanced economy. Several factors helped. Its big banks had less exposure to American subprime securities and were closely supervised. The Reserve Bank cut the cash rate rapidly, from 7.25 per cent in September 2008 to 3 per cent by April 2009. The government guaranteed bank deposits and wholesale funding, and spent heavily on stimulus, including cash payments to households and a large school building program. Meanwhile China launched its own enormous stimulus, keeping demand for Australian iron ore and coal strong. The economy shrank in only one quarter, at the end of 2008, so it avoided the technical definition of a recession, two consecutive quarters of decline."],
    ["The long shadow", "The crisis led to tougher bank rules and years of low interest rates and slow growth.",
      "Elsewhere, the damage lasted years. Millions of Americans lost their homes, unemployment in the United States reached around 10 per cent, and parts of Europe slid into a debt crisis from 2010, with Greece the most severe case. Central banks cut interest rates to near zero and began buying bonds on a vast scale. Regulators responded with new rules. The international Basel III standards required banks to hold more and better quality capital and more liquid assets, and the US passed the Dodd-Frank Act in 2010. Debate continues over whether these reforms went far enough, and over the fairness of rescuing banks while many ordinary people bore the costs. The crisis also fuelled lasting public anger at finance."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What were subprime mortgages?", ["Loans only for commercial property", "Home loans to borrowers with weak credit histories", "Mortgages guaranteed by the government", "Loans with no interest"], 1, "Subprime loans went to riskier borrowers, often with teaser rates that later rose."],
    [1, "What is securitisation?", ["Hiring security guards for banks", "Bundling loans into securities sold to investors", "Insuring deposits", "Raising interest rates"], 1, "Banks bundled mortgages into securities and sold them, spreading the risk worldwide."],
    [2, "Why did leverage make banks so vulnerable?", ["They had no customers", "Heavy borrowing meant a small fall in asset values could wipe out their capital", "It forced them to hold gold", "It made their shares too expensive"], 1, "With thin capital and large borrowings, modest losses could make a bank insolvent."],
    [3, "What happened on 15 September 2008?", ["Bear Stearns was sold", "Lehman Brothers filed for bankruptcy", "TARP was repaid", "The Basel III rules took effect"], 1, "Lehman's bankruptcy, the largest in US history, turned the crisis into a global panic."],
    [4, "Which of these helped Australia avoid recession during the crisis?", ["Raising interest rates sharply", "Large exposure to US subprime securities", "Rapid rate cuts, government stimulus and strong Chinese demand", "Leaving the gold standard"], 2, "Fast rate cuts, stimulus, sound banks and China's demand for resources all helped."]
  ],
  { added: "2026-10-26" });

WB.L("market-for-lemons", "money", "intermediate", "The market for lemons: when sellers know more than buyers", "A short paper about dodgy used cars was rejected by several journals. It went on to win a Nobel Prize and explain why some markets break down.",
  [
    ["Peaches and lemons", "In 1970 George Akerlof showed what happens when sellers know far more than buyers.",
      "In the United States a defective car is called a lemon, while a good one is sometimes called a peach. In 1970 a young economist named George Akerlof published a paper titled The Market for Lemons, using used cars to explore a simple but powerful idea. The seller of a used car knows its history: whether it has been in a flood, burns oil or was thrashed by its owner. The buyer usually does not. Economists call this asymmetric information, where one side of a deal knows much more than the other. Akerlof asked what happens to a market when buyers cannot tell good products from bad ones. The answer was surprisingly gloomy."],
    ["How a market unravels", "If buyers cannot tell good from bad, good sellers may leave and the market shrinks.",
      "Imagine buyers know that some used cars are peaches and some are lemons, but cannot tell which is which. Sensibly, they will only offer an average price. To the owner of a genuine peach, that average is too low, so many of them decide not to sell. That leaves a higher share of lemons on the market. Buyers notice and lower their offers further, which drives out even more good cars. In the extreme, the market can collapse until only lemons remain, or no trade happens at all, even though many buyers would have happily paid a fair price for a good car. Bad quality, in effect, drives out good."],
    ["Rejected, then rewarded", "Akerlof's paper was turned down several times before becoming a classic.",
      "The paper had a rocky start. Akerlof later recounted that it was rejected by several leading journals, with reviewers suggesting it was too trivial or, alternatively, that if it were correct, economics would be different. It was eventually published in the Quarterly Journal of Economics in 1970. Over time it became one of the most cited papers in the field, because the idea applied far beyond cars. In 2001 Akerlof shared the Nobel Prize in economics with Michael Spence and Joseph Stiglitz, who had developed related ideas about how markets cope with hidden information. Together their work helped launch the economics of information, which now underpins thinking about insurance, lending and online trade."],
    ["Insurance and adverse selection", "People who expect to claim are keener to buy insurance, which can push premiums up.",
      "Insurance shows the lemons problem in reverse. The buyer of health insurance often knows more about their own health than the insurer does. If premiums are set at an average level, people who expect to need lots of care find the deal attractive, while healthy people may decide it is not worth it. As the healthy leave, the insured pool gets sicker and more expensive, premiums rise and still more healthy people drop out. This spiral is called adverse selection. Australia's Lifetime Health Cover, introduced in 2000, tackles it by adding a loading to private hospital cover premiums for people who first take out cover later in life, from their early thirties on, encouraging people to join while they are young and healthy."],
    ["Signals and screens", "Markets fight back with warranties, credentials and checks that reveal hidden quality.",
      "Markets are not helpless. Michael Spence showed how the informed side can send a signal, an action that is costly for low-quality sellers to fake. A dealer who offers a long warranty on a used car is signalling confidence, since honouring it on a lemon would be expensive. Spence's best-known example was education: a degree may signal persistence and ability to employers, whatever its direct usefulness. Stiglitz showed how the less informed side can screen, for instance by offering a menu of insurance policies with different excesses so that customers reveal their own risk by what they choose. Brand reputations, independent inspections and vehicle history reports all perform the same job, making hidden quality visible."],
    ["Lemons everywhere", "From online reviews to consumer law, much of modern commerce exists to solve information gaps.",
      "Once you see the lemons problem, it pops up everywhere. Online marketplaces rely on ratings and reviews so strangers can trust each other, and fake reviews are damaging precisely because they poison those signals. Lenders check credit histories because borrowers know more about their own finances. Employers ask for references. Consumer protection laws, including statutory guarantees that goods must be of acceptable quality, give buyers some assurance and reduce the advantage of sellers who hide defects. Akerlof's insight was that missing information is not a minor detail. It can stop a market working at all. Much of the machinery of modern commerce, from warranties to watchdogs, exists to close that gap."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What is asymmetric information?", ["When prices are different in two countries", "When one side of a deal knows much more than the other", "When both sides share all information", "When information is protected by copyright"], 1, "Akerlof used used cars, where sellers know more than buyers, to illustrate asymmetric information."],
    [1, "In Akerlof's model, why might good cars disappear from the market?", ["Buyers prefer lemons", "Average prices are too low for owners of good cars, so they stop selling", "The government bans them", "Good cars are always exported"], 1, "When buyers only offer average prices, owners of good cars withdraw, leaving more lemons."],
    [3, "What is adverse selection in insurance?", ["Insurers choosing only healthy customers", "People most likely to claim being keenest to buy cover, pushing premiums up", "Choosing the wrong policy by mistake", "A ban on insurance for young people"], 1, "As healthy people drop out, the pool gets riskier and premiums rise, a spiral called adverse selection."],
    [4, "Why can a long warranty work as a signal?", ["It is required by law", "It would be very costly for a seller of a bad car to honour", "It lowers the car's price", "It hides the car's history"], 1, "A signal works because it is costly to fake, so offering it suggests genuine quality."],
    [2, "Who shared the 2001 Nobel Prize in economics with Akerlof?", ["Keynes and Hayek", "Kahneman and Thaler", "Michael Spence and Joseph Stiglitz", "Diamond and Dybvig"], 2, "Spence and Stiglitz developed related ideas about signalling and screening under hidden information."]
  ],
  { added: "2026-10-26" });

WB.L("measuring-inequality", "money", "intermediate", "Inequality: how the gap between rich and poor is measured", "Is the gap between rich and poor growing? The answer depends on what you measure, and economists have been arguing about it for more than a century.",
  [
    ["Income versus wealth", "Income is what flows in each year; wealth is what you own, and it is usually far more unequal.",
      "Talk about inequality often blurs two different things. Income is the money that comes in over a period, from wages, business profits, interest, rent or government payments. Wealth is the stock of what you own minus what you owe: a house, superannuation, shares, savings and a car, less any mortgage or debts. A young doctor might have a high income but little wealth, while a retired farmer could have modest income and substantial wealth in land. Across almost every country, wealth is distributed far more unequally than income. Wealth builds up over a lifetime, earns its own returns and can be passed on to the next generation, so differences tend to compound over time."],
    ["The Lorenz curve and Gini coefficient", "A single number from 0 to 1 summarises how unequally income or wealth is shared.",
      "In 1905 the American economist Max Lorenz proposed a neat way to picture inequality. Line everyone up from poorest to richest and plot what share of total income the bottom 10 per cent, 20 per cent and so on receive. If everyone earned the same, the line would be perfectly straight. The more it sags, the more unequal the society. In 1912 the Italian statistician Corrado Gini turned this into a single number. A Gini coefficient of 0 means perfect equality, everyone has the same, and 1 means one person has everything. Most developed countries have income Gini figures somewhere between roughly 0.25 and 0.40, and wealth figures that are considerably higher."],
    ["Pareto's surprising pattern", "Vilfredo Pareto noticed that wealth tends to concentrate in similar ways across very different societies.",
      "Around 1900 the Italian economist Vilfredo Pareto studied tax and property records from several countries and centuries. He noticed that the distribution of income and wealth at the top followed a similar mathematical pattern again and again, with a small share of people holding a large share of the total. The popular 80/20 rule was later named after him, although that rule of thumb is far looser than his actual mathematics. Pareto's pattern, now called a power law, still describes the top of wealth distributions surprisingly well, and it means averages can be misleading when a few people hold so much."],
    ["Median, not mean", "When a few people are very rich, the average can paint a misleadingly rosy picture.",
      "If a billionaire walks into a pub, the average wealth of the drinkers suddenly soars, yet nobody else is any richer. This is why economists often prefer the median, the person exactly in the middle when everyone is lined up, to the mean, or average. In unequal societies the mean income sits well above the median, because a small group of high earners pulls it up. Statisticians also compare ratios, such as the income of someone at the 90th percentile with someone at the 10th. Another common approach is to track the share of total income going to the top 1 per cent. Each measure highlights a different part of the picture, so careful analysts look at several."],
    ["Kuznets, Piketty and the great debate", "Economists disagree about whether growth naturally narrows or widens inequality.",
      "In 1955 economist Simon Kuznets suggested that inequality rises as countries industrialise and then falls as they mature, an idea pictured as an upside-down U, the Kuznets curve. For a while the mid-twentieth century seemed to prove him right, as gaps narrowed in many rich countries. Then, from around 1980, top income shares rose again in the United States, Britain and elsewhere. In 2013 French economist Thomas Piketty published Capital in the Twenty-First Century, arguing that when the return on capital exceeds the growth rate of the economy, wealth tends to concentrate. His book became a surprise bestseller and drew strong criticism, with economists still debating his data, his theory and his proposed wealth taxes."],
    ["Mobility: can you climb the ladder?", "How easily people move up or down the income ladder matters as much as the gap itself.",
      "A snapshot of inequality does not say whether the same people stay at the top or bottom. Economic mobility asks how strongly your income as an adult is linked to your parents' income. Research led by economist Miles Corak found that countries with higher inequality tend to have lower mobility between generations, a link dubbed the Great Gatsby curve by Alan Krueger in 2012. The relationship is a correlation, and researchers debate what drives it, from education and housing to social networks. Measured mobility in Australia has generally been found to be higher than in the United States, though comparing countries is tricky. Many people care less about gaps themselves than about whether the ladder is climbable."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why is wealth usually more unequal than income?", ["Wealth is not taxed", "It builds up over lifetimes, earns returns and can be inherited", "Income is fixed by law", "Wealth only includes cash"], 1, "Wealth accumulates and compounds across lifetimes and generations, so gaps widen over time."],
    [1, "What does a Gini coefficient of 0 mean?", ["One person has everything", "The data are missing", "Everyone has exactly the same", "The economy is not growing"], 2, "0 is perfect equality and 1 means one person holds everything."],
    [3, "Why do economists often prefer the median to the mean when measuring typical income?", ["The median is easier to calculate", "A few very high earners can pull the mean well above what a typical person earns", "The mean is illegal to publish", "The median always rises faster"], 1, "The median shows the person in the middle, unaffected by extreme incomes at the top."],
    [4, "What did Thomas Piketty argue in Capital in the Twenty-First Century?", ["Inequality always falls as economies grow", "When returns on capital exceed economic growth, wealth tends to concentrate", "Money should be backed by gold", "Inequality cannot be measured"], 1, "Piketty argued that when the return on capital exceeds growth, wealth concentrates, a view still debated."],
    [5, "What does the Great Gatsby curve describe?", ["Rising house prices in New York", "A link between higher inequality and lower mobility across generations", "The spending habits of the very rich", "The shape of the Lorenz curve"], 1, "Countries with more inequality tend to have less mobility between parents' and children's incomes."]
  ],
  { added: "2026-10-26" });

WB.L("quantitative-easing", "money", "advanced", "Quantitative easing: what central banks do when rates hit zero", "When interest rates can fall no further, central banks reach for a stranger tool: creating money to buy bonds. Australia tried it in 2020, with mixed results.",
  [
    ["The zero lower bound", "Central banks normally steer the economy with short-term interest rates, but those can only fall so far.",
      "In ordinary times a central bank like the Reserve Bank of Australia manages the economy mainly through its cash rate, the overnight interest rate at which banks lend to each other. Cutting it makes borrowing cheaper and encourages spending and investment. But there is a floor. Once rates approach zero, cutting further becomes difficult, because people and banks could simply hold cash, which pays nothing but never charges you either. Economists call this the zero lower bound. Some central banks in Europe and Japan pushed slightly negative rates, but not by much. When a severe downturn hits and rates are already near zero, central banks need other tools. Quantitative easing, usually shortened to QE, is the best known."],
    ["How QE works", "A central bank creates new reserves and uses them to buy bonds, pushing down longer-term interest rates.",
      "Under QE a central bank buys large amounts of financial assets, mainly government bonds, from banks and investors. It pays for them by creating new central bank reserves, electronic money held in the accounts that commercial banks keep at the central bank. No banknotes are printed, despite the popular phrase. The purchases push up bond prices, and because bond prices and yields move in opposite directions, longer-term interest rates fall. Lower long-term rates feed through to mortgages and business loans. Sellers of bonds receive cash they may invest in other assets such as corporate bonds or shares, an effect known as portfolio rebalancing. QE also signals that the central bank intends to keep rates low for a long time."],
    ["From Tokyo to the world", "Japan pioneered QE in 2001, and other major central banks adopted it after 2008.",
      "Japan was the first to try modern QE. After its asset bubble burst in the early 1990s, the country endured years of weak growth and falling prices. In 2001 the Bank of Japan began buying government bonds to expand bank reserves, a program that ran until 2006 and was later revived on a much larger scale. The global financial crisis made QE mainstream. From late 2008 the US Federal Reserve bought trillions of dollars of government bonds and mortgage-backed securities over several rounds, and the Bank of England began its own program in 2009. The European Central Bank launched large-scale bond buying in 2015. What began as an emergency experiment became a standard part of the toolkit."],
    ["Australia's 2020 experiment", "During the pandemic the RBA cut rates to near zero, targeted bond yields and bought bonds for the first time.",
      "Australia avoided QE during the global financial crisis because its interest rates were well above zero. The pandemic changed that. In March 2020 the Reserve Bank cut the cash rate to 0.25 per cent and introduced a target for the yield on three-year government bonds, also 0.25 per cent, promising to buy whatever was needed to hold it there. It also offered banks cheap three-year funding through a Term Funding Facility. In November 2020 it cut the cash rate and yield target to 0.1 per cent and announced a Bond Purchase Program of 100 billion dollars, later extended. Over the following year and a half, purchases under that program eventually totalled roughly 280 billion dollars."],
    ["The yield target that broke", "Australia's yield target was abandoned abruptly in 2021, damaging the RBA's credibility.",
      "Yield control is not new. The US Federal Reserve capped government bond yields during and after the Second World War until 1951, and the Bank of Japan adopted a version in 2016. Australia's target, though, ended awkwardly. As the economy recovered faster than expected in 2021 and inflation began to rise, markets bet that rates would rise sooner than the RBA had suggested. In late October 2021 the bank stopped defending the target, and in early November formally abandoned it. Many borrowers had taken the bank's earlier guidance that rates would likely stay low until 2024 at face value. The bank's own review in 2022 acknowledged that the disorderly exit had damaged its reputation."],
    ["Did QE work, and at what cost?", "QE probably helped in crises, but its effects are hard to measure and its side effects are debated.",
      "Most central bank research concludes that QE lowered long-term interest rates and helped stabilise markets during crises, especially when markets were in turmoil. Measuring its effect on jobs and growth is much harder, because nobody can rerun history without it. Critics raise several concerns. By pushing up the prices of bonds, shares and property, QE may have widened wealth gaps, since those assets are mostly owned by people who are already wealthy. It can blur the line between monetary policy and government financing. And when interest rates later rose, central banks faced large losses on the bonds they held. The RBA reported negative equity after its losses in 2022, though central banks can keep operating normally in that situation."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What is the zero lower bound?", ["The minimum wage", "The limit on how far interest rates can usefully be cut", "The lowest possible exchange rate", "A legal limit on government debt"], 1, "Once rates are near zero, further cuts are difficult because people can hold cash instead."],
    [1, "How does a central bank pay for bonds under QE?", ["By printing large quantities of banknotes", "By raising taxes", "By creating new central bank reserves electronically", "By selling gold"], 2, "QE is funded with newly created reserves held by commercial banks at the central bank."],
    [2, "Which central bank pioneered modern QE in 2001?", ["The Bank of Japan", "The Reserve Bank of Australia", "The European Central Bank", "The Bank of England"], 0, "Japan began buying bonds to expand reserves in 2001 after years of weak growth and falling prices."],
    [3, "What did the RBA target in March 2020 besides the cash rate?", ["The price of iron ore", "The exchange rate with the US dollar", "The yield on three-year government bonds", "House prices"], 2, "The RBA set a 0.25 per cent target for three-year government bond yields."],
    [5, "Which is a common criticism of QE?", ["It always causes hyperinflation", "It may widen wealth gaps by lifting asset prices", "It lowers bond prices", "It cannot be used by central banks"], 1, "Higher prices for shares, bonds and property mainly benefit people who already own those assets."]
  ],
  { added: "2026-10-26" });
